A||||4||rodmcneill||Pioneer tri-axial 4x10||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||03-06-2001||01:19 PM||||Has anyone used the Pioneer tri-axial 4x10 speakers? I was wondering if they were worth the extra money over the co-axials.How is sound quality, noticably different?||||reg||5
Z||000001||286SE'S||03-06-2001||02:33 PM||||I have the co-axials in my 2M6 with a stock premiums sound system and the tri-axials in the 2M4 with a Pioneer DEH-76DH. The overall sound is different because of the head units but I do not think the added midrange in the tri-axials was worth the extra $$. The speakers sound very similar. The midrange is very good in both but the Delco system lacks high end and seems to have more low bass because of the sub. (thanks rodney!).<BR>Both were a big improvment over the rotted out originals. Had some crappy Pyramid speakers in the 2M4 for a week. Nasty, shrill sound and the volume was very low. I gave them away.||||reg||1
Z||000002||LarryB||03-06-2001||03:01 PM||||I have the tri-axial units in both my Fieros, they're a huge improvement over the stock units, even over the performance sound speakers.  I'll defer to 286SE'S as to the 4105's being worth the additional money over the 4103's but I'm sure you'd easily notice how much better either one sounds over the Delcos. <P>The Pyramid's are garbage, they just plain suck.  Better off staying with the stock speakers and saving your pennies until you can afford a set of Pioneers, Kenwoods, Rockford Fosgates or == than waste your money on that junk. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||overlord||03-06-2001||07:49 PM||||speaking of speakers, (no sense in making a separate topic)  can i hook a pair of tweeteres directly to my  headunit?<BR>Like how my normal speakers are without going to an external amplfier?<P>I read somewhere that tweeters are super easy to blow cause of their sensitivity and should always be hooked up to an amp or something like that.<P>I was thinking of just splicing into my front speaker wire and have a quick easy install for some nice  HIGHS||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||03-06-2001||10:16 PM||||Well, depending on a couple of things, you *could*.  Whether or not you liked the results is a different story. This is kind of a long explanation so bear with me. <P>1) You're actually more likely to blow a tweeter by overdriving a lower-powered amplifier past its rated output (causing a phenomenon known as 'clipping', where the peaks of the waveforms are sheared off because the amp can't deliver enough power, distorting the sound) than you are by driving the tweeter past its rated power handling capacity but using a higher-powered amp delivering nice clean power. <P>2) You need to account for the rated impedance that the tweeter and the existing speaker if you're going to splice in a tweeter.  If you wire them in series the effect is additive (e.g. 4 ohms + 4 ohms = 8 ohms) but if you wire them in parallel the effect is ((4*4)/(4+4)) = 16/8 = 2 ohms.  The actual formulas are R(equiv) = (R1 + R2) if in series, R(equiv) = (R1*R2)/(R1+R2) in parallel. Most of the better head units like a Pioneer, Kenwood, Alpine, JVC, etc. use amplifiers that generally can correctly handle 2-8 ohm impedances, and some of the really better ones can handle 1 ohm safely. If you have a good amplifier you can probably get away with doing what you suggest.  If you have a Sony/Jensen/Blaupunkt I'd think twice about it. <P>3) You also have to take into account that tweeters by design don't handle lower frequencies very well.  If you don't use a crossover or high-pass filter (Bass Blockers are a type of high-pass filter) you'll be at the least wasting energy trying to drive them at frequencies they by design just can't handle well.  At worst you'll overdrive (or overheat) and potentially damage the tweeter. <P>So, if you want to experiment and/or do some up front design work you can make what you're suggesting work really well.  Or, you could design something using off-the-shelf component pairs like from Polk or Boston Acoustics.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I personally would either be happy with off-the-shelf 2- or 3-way speakers (which is what I actually did for my Fieros) or do some careful planning and install component speakers. Either way I think the results are more predictable than just splicing in random pieces. <BR>||||reg||1
